  "tagline": "2013 open-source in-memory distributed OLTP RDBMS speaking the PostgreSQL protocol; abandoned in 2014.",
  "description": "InfiniSQL was an attempt by Mark Travis to build a relational database that scales transaction processing across many hosts without application sharding. Each node runs a multithreaded, actor-style engine holding all data in memory, transactions can span nodes, and clients connect over PostgreSQL's frontend/backend protocol v3, so ordinary PostgreSQL drivers work. It supported ad-hoc SQL, multi-statement transactions and stored procedures, and its launch in late 2013 claimed over 500,000 complex transactions per second on 12 servers. The catch: data was never written to disk, and the planned synchronous replicas and hybrid memory/disk storage were never implemented. Commits stopped in early 2014.",
